{"gid":"2026_04_KC_LV","week":4,"away":"KC","home":"LV","away_col":"#FF4D57","home_col":"#C9D2DC","spread":-5.5,"total":43.5,"fav":"KC","hpts":19,"apts":24,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 4 \u00b7 16:25","roof":"Dome","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Travis Kelce</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 LV are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>The <strong>LV</strong> welcome the <strong>KC</strong> as underdogs of <strong>5.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>43.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>KC 24\u201319</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>40% of its snaps with KC in front</strong>, <strong>36% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with KC chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>A <strong>modest edge to KC</strong>. They should lead more often than not, tilting them slightly toward the run late, while LV leans a touch more pass-heavy to stay in it \u2014 but the effect is gentle; this isn't a script that dramatically reshapes anyone's usage.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p><strong>Patrick Mahomes</strong> adds rushing value \u2014 he scrambles on 4.8% of dropbacks, live for rush-yard and anytime-TD props.</p><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Ashton Jeanty</strong> \u2014 KC are stingy against the run (108 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 KC hold WR1s to just 50 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Travis Kelce</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 LV are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 LV surrender 0.44 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone LV throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Tre Tucker</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to KC \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (24 carries, 105 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though KC's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>10 targets and 90 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 71 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>LV \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LV are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (46% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 LV project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Ashton Jeanty</strong>'s rushing projection (21 carries, 76 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, LV funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>11 targets for 84 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brock Bowers</strong> (TE, 14 targets, 111 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"KC","col":"#FF4D57","players":[{"name":"Patrick Mahomes","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":257.41,"book":238.5,"over_th":239.74,"under_th":266.49,"over_am":"\u2212115","under_am":"\u2212115","pover":56,"call":"none","ev":3.8,"edge":18.9,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.89,"book":1.5,"over_th":1.27,"under_th":1.46,"over_am":"\u2212145","under_am":"+111","pover":44,"ca